<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Minaret of Jam</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1190s</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Minaret of Jam is a brick minaret about 65 metres high in a remote valley of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghor">Ghor</a> province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, beside the Hari Rud. Built by the Ghurid sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghiyath-al-din-muhammad">Ghiyath al-Din Muhammad</a>, probably in the 1190s, it is covered with geometric brickwork and the Qur'anic chapter of Maryam. It probably marks the site of the Ghurid capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firozkoh">Firozkoh</a>, and in 2002 it became Afghanistan's first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/minaret-of-jam.jpg" alt="Minaret of Jam" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: david adamec · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jam_afghan_architecture_harirud_brick.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
